# Paths whose modification demands deliberate review, not a reflexive approval.
#
# One path per line, like an allowlist. The text after "#" is the reason shown
# to the reviewer when the tripwire fires, so write it as the thing to look
# for, not a label - "this is sensitive" tells nobody anything they had not
# already assumed. Blank lines and whole-line comments are ignored.
#
# Deliberately not YAML. This is read by a shell script in CI and by a Go test,
# and a format that needs a parser is a format that needs a dependency; the one
# obvious choice for that job, tj-actions/changed-files, is the action that got
# backdoored in 2025 to scrape secrets out of runner logs. grep and cut do not
# have supply chains.
#
# What belongs here is narrower than "important". The test is whether a change
# could remove a safety property WHILE LOOKING ROUTINE IN A DIFF. A deleted
# assertion is three green lines and a smaller file. Things that break loudly -
# a bad provider, a malformed manifest - already fail CI and need no shouting.
#
# tests/go/repo/sensitivepaths_test.go checks this file: every path must exist,
# every path must carry a reason, and every code law must be covered.

tests/go/repo/                              # The code laws. Deleting one assertion is a smaller file and a green build.
.github/sensitive-paths                     # This list. Removing a line turns the alarm off for that path, silently.
.github/workflows/                          # Runs with a token. The agent cannot write here, so a human meant this.
.github/actions/                            # Every job's checkout and pinned versions. A change here reaches every lane at once.
.pre-commit-config.yaml                     # Owns formatting and the commit-msg hook, pinned exactly. Unpinning resolves to whatever is latest.

config/management.tpl.json                  # The vault contract. An unwrapped op:// reference reaches a provider as literal text.
management/cluster/registry.tf              # The invariants that refuse a bad config. Removing one does not fail - it stops refusing.

scripts/steward/internal/onepassword/        # Every secret passes here. probe.go returns a Status, never the value it read.
scripts/steward/internal/phases/health.go    # Gates Migrate, the point of no return. Weakening it reports success over a cluster that never converged.
scripts/steward/internal/phases/backup.go    # Pipes state into age without plaintext touching disk. Buffering to a file breaks that.
scripts/steward/internal/phases/restore.go   # The only place that reads the break-glass private key.
scripts/steward/internal/phases/encryption.go# Sets TF_ENCRYPTION. Wrong here writes plaintext state to a database that ships off-site.
scripts/steward/internal/phases/sterilize.go # Wipes every secret on exit. Skipping a file leaves secrets on disk and still reports success.
scripts/steward/internal/phases/teardown.go  # Destroys real infrastructure. The ordering stops state being wiped before the VMs it describes.
scripts/signedpush/                         # Reads the GitHub App private key. Reaching this mints tokens for this repository.
